### What is the Longest Word in Dictionary problem?

Given a list of words, find the longest word that can be built one character at a time by adding one letter to the end, where every intermediate string is also a word in the list. If there are ties, return the lexicographically smallest. For example, given `["A", "AP", "APP", "APPL", "APPLE", "B", "BA", "BAT"]`, the answer is `"APPLE"` because every prefix (`"A"`, `"AP"`, `"APP"`, `"APPL"`) is also in the list.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Words: ["A", "AP", "APP", "APPL", "APPLE", "B", "BA", "BAT"]
// "APPLE" -> prefixes: "A", "AP", "APP", "APPL" -> all in the list
// "BAT"   -> prefixes: "B", "BA" -> all in the list, but length 3 < 5
// Answer: "APPLE"
```

### Why use a trie?

The key insight is that a word is buildable one character at a time exactly when every node on its path from the root is marked as a complete word. A trie naturally represents this: after inserting all words and marking their end nodes, you traverse the trie from the root and only descend through nodes that are marked as words. This ensures every prefix is valid.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Trie after inserting ["A", "AP", "APP"]:
// root -> A[word] -> P[word] -> P[word]
// DFS from root: A is a word -> descend
// A -> P is a word -> descend
// AP -> P is a word -> descend
// APP is reachable with all-word path, length 3
```

### How do you implement it?

Build the trie by inserting all words. Then run a DFS from the root, only stepping into children that are marked as complete words. Track the longest word found, breaking ties by lexicographic order.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LongestWordInDictionary {

    private static class TrieNode {
        TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26];
        String word = null;
    }

    private TrieNode root = new TrieNode();
    private String best = "";

    public String longestWord(String[] words) {
        // Build trie
        for (String w : words) {
            TrieNode node = root;
            for (char c : w.toCharArray()) {
                int idx = c - 'a';
                if (node.children[idx] == null) {
                    node.children[idx] = new TrieNode();
                }
                node = node.children[idx];
            }
            node.word = w;
        }

        // DFS only through word nodes
        best = "";
        dfs(root);
        return best;
    }

    private void dfs(TrieNode node) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
            TrieNode child = node.children[i];
            if (child != null && child.word != null) {
                // This child is a complete word -> descend
                if (child.word.length() > best.length()) {
                    best = child.word;
                }
                dfs(child);
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LongestWordInDictionary solver = new LongestWordInDictionary();
        System.out.println(solver.longestWord(
            new String[]{"a", "ap", "app", "appl", "apple",
                          "b", "ba", "bat"}));
        System.out.println(solver.longestWord(
            new String[]{"w", "wo", "wor", "worl", "world"}));
    }
}
```

```text
apple
world
```

### How does the DFS enforce the buildable rule?

The DFS only enters a child node if it is marked as a complete word. This means every node on the path from root to the current position represents a valid word in the list. If any intermediate prefix is missing, that node will not be marked as a word, and the DFS will not descend through it, stopping the path.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Words: ["A", "APP"] (missing "AP")
// Trie: root -> A[word] -> P[not word] -> P[word]
// DFS: root -> A (word, descend)
//      A -> P (NOT a word, do not descend!)
// "APP" is never reached because "AP" is missing
// This correctly enforces the one-char-at-a-time rule
```

### How do you handle the lexicographic tie-break?

By iterating over children in order from index 0 (character 'a') to index 25 (character 'z'), the DFS visits lexicographically smaller children first. When two words have the same length, the first one found (the lexicographically smaller one) is kept because the update condition checks strictly greater length.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Words: ["a", "ab", "ac"]
// Both "ab" and "ac" have length 2
// DFS visits 'b' before 'c' (index order)
// "ab" is found first, best = "ab"
// "ac" has same length, 2 > 2 is false, best stays "ab"
// Correct: "ab" < "ac" lexicographically
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include: no word of length 1 (no word is buildable, return empty), all single-character words, duplicate words in the list, and very long chains.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Edge case 1: no single-char words -> no word is buildable, return ""
// Edge case 2: ["a", "b", "c"] -> longest buildable is "a" (or "b" or "c")
//              with tie-break: "a" (smallest)
// Edge case 3: ["a", "a"] -> duplicates handled by trie naturally
// Edge case 4: ["a","ab","abc",...,"abcdef..."] -> long chain
```

### What about using BFS instead of DFS?

BFS from the root is an alternative: enqueue children that are complete words, processing level by level. The last word dequeued at the deepest level is the answer (with appropriate tie-break). BFS naturally finds the longest path because it processes shorter words first.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;
import java.util.Set;

public class LongestWordBFS {

    public String longestWord(String[] words) {
        Arrays.sort(words); // sort for tie-break
        Set<String> built = new HashSet<>();
        built.add(""); // empty string is trivially buildable
        String best = "";

        for (String word : words) {
            String prefix = word.substring(0, word.length() - 1);
            if (built.contains(prefix)) {
                built.add(word);
                if (word.length() > best.length()) {
                    best = word;
                }
            }
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

### What about a banking use case?

At the National Bank of India, product feature codes are built incrementally. A longer code is only valid if every shorter prefix is a registered code. Rajesh finds the longest valid code chain.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class FeatureCodeChain {

    private final LongestWordInDictionary solver = new LongestWordInDictionary();

    public String findLongestChain(String[] registeredCodes) {
        String longest = solver.longestWord(registeredCodes);
        System.out.println("Longest buildable code: " + longest);
        return longest;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FeatureCodeChain chain = new FeatureCodeChain();
        chain.findLongestChain(new String[]{
            "p", "pa", "pay", "paym", "payme", "paymen", "payment"});
    }
}
```

```text
Longest buildable code: payment
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that this is a traversal application of the Trie pattern, distinct from search applications like Word Search II. The word-only descent rule is the structural encoding of the buildable constraint, and sorted child traversal handles the tie-break cleanly. The alternative sort-and-set approach is simpler but uses more space; the trie approach is more efficient for large dictionaries.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Senior notes:
// 1. Trie traversal, not search: the constraint is structural
// 2. Word-only descent = buildable-one-char-at-a-time constraint
// 3. Sorted child iteration = free lexicographic tie-break
// 4. Time: O(total characters) for build + traverse
// 5. Alternative: sort words + HashSet of buildable, O(n*L*log(n))
```
